VALIDATED

## What is proven

For every finite MPY string whose character codes are in `0..127`, loading the
two exact function bodies translated from `solution.py` and calling
`anti_shuffle` returns the words in their original order, with every literal
space preserved in place and every word's characters in nondecreasing code
order. This is a partial-correctness result in the Kit sense.

The entry computation is the exact
`#loadAll(Module(FuncDef("insert_char", ...) FuncDef("anti_shuffle", ...)))`
term followed by `Call(Name("anti_shuffle"), str(CODES))`. The constrained
observable is the returned string. The proof also fixes normal return/control
state (`env`, `scopeLoc`, `heap`, `heapLoc`, `stack`, `ret`, `exc`, and exit
code); the final module scope is intentionally unobserved.

## Formal claim

`SPEC-ENTRY.anti-shuffle` requires `asciiCodes(CODES)` and reaches:

```k
<k> str(antiFinish(.IntSeq, .IntSeq, CODES)) </k>
```

`insertFinish(PREFIX,C,SUFFIX)` inserts `C` immediately before the first
greater character (or at the end). `antiFinish(OUT,WORD,REM)` emits one space
for each input space and uses that insertion operation for every non-space
character. By induction on `SUFFIX`, insertion preserves both multiplicity and
nondecreasing order. By induction on `REM`, completed words and spaces are
preserved while the current word remains ordered. Thus the exact constructive
postcondition has the prompt's human-facing meaning.

The helper loop is proved in `SPEC-HELPER.insert-loop`; the sentence loop is
proved in `SPEC.anti-loop`; the entry claim is proved last.

